{%- comment -%}
  Normalised "finish family" key for a product: the part of its title that is
  identical across every finish of the same model, so two products can be
  compared to see whether they are the same machine in a different finish.

  Preferred source:

    - custom.combined_listing_family, when the product has an explicit family
      key. This supports sibling products whose model names differ, such as the
      Telluride and Vail Hip Thrust / Glute Machines.

  Legacy fallback:

    - the finish word, from custom.product_color ("Black", "Silver", ...)
    - the series code that carries the finish, taken as the first segment of
      custom.product_code ("FFB-DAP" -> "FFB", "FFS-DAP" -> "FFS")

  The fallback preserves the existing DAP-style title matching. The series code
  has to come out too: those products are titled "French Fitness FFB Black
  <Type>" / "French Fitness FFS Silver <Type>", so removing only the colour
  word would leave FFB vs FFS behind and the two finishes of one machine would
  never match. Codes whose prefix is not in the title (FFM-PLISPL, and any other
  series that does not put its code in the title) are left untouched.

  Fallback values are only removed as whole space-delimited words, so a colour
  or code that happens to be part of a longer word is left alone.

  Accepts:
    product - {Object} the product to build the key for (required)
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- liquid
  assign explicit_family = product.metafields.custom.combined_listing_family.value

  if explicit_family != blank
    echo explicit_family | strip | downcase | split: ' ' | join: ' '
  else
    assign color = product.metafields.custom.product_color.value
    assign code = product.metafields.custom.product_code.value

    assign key = ' ' | append: product.title | append: ' '

    if color != blank
      assign color_word = ' ' | append: color | append: ' '
      assign key = key | replace: color_word, ' '
    endif

    if code != blank
      assign series = code | split: '-' | first
      if series != blank
        assign series_word = ' ' | append: series | append: ' '
        assign key = key | replace: series_word, ' '
      endif
    endif

    echo key | downcase | split: ' ' | join: ' '
  endif
-%}
